\section{Introduction to Model Validation}
% ============================================================================

\begin{keypoint}{Why Validation Matters}
\textbf{Model validation answers the critical question:} Can we trust this model's predictions for new, unseen data?

A model that perfectly fits training data but performs poorly on new data has \textit{overfit}---it memorized noise rather than learned true patterns. Validation ensures our models are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Generalizable:} Perform well on future outbreaks, not just historical data
    \item \textbf{Calibrated:} Predicted probabilities match observed frequencies
    \item \textbf{Reliable:} Performance is stable across different data subsets
    \item \textbf{Trustworthy:} Decisions based on model predictions are sound
\end{enumerate}
\end{keypoint}

\subsection{Validation Strategy Overview}

We employ four complementary validation approaches, each addressing different aspects of model performance:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Four Validation Methods: What Each Tests}
\label{tab:validation_overview}
\begin{tabular}{p{4cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{What It Tests} & \textbf{What It Detects} \\
\midrule
\textbf{10-Fold Cross-Validation} & Stability across data subsets & Overfitting to specific training samples \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{70/30 Train-Test Split} & Performance on completely unseen data & Overfitting to training set \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{Calibration Assessment} & Accuracy of predicted probabilities & Probability miscalibration \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{ROC \& Threshold Analysis} & Discrimination ability \& optimal cutoffs & Classification performance trade-offs \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{detail}{Why Multiple Validation Methods?}
\textbf{No single metric captures all aspects of model quality.} Each validation method provides complementary information:

\textbf{Cross-Validation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Uses all data efficiently (no single test set ``wasted'')
    \item Provides mean and variance of performance
    \item Tests stability across data partitions
    \item \textbf{Limitation:} All data used in some fold, so not truly independent
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Train-Test Split:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Truly independent test set (completely unseen)
    \item Simulates prediction on future data
    \item Provides unbiased performance estimate
    \item \textbf{Limitation:} Single split; results depend on which data in test set
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Calibration:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ensures predicted probabilities are meaningful
    \item Critical for risk-based decision making
    \item \textbf{Example:} If model predicts 70\% risk, do 7 out of 10 such cases truly have outbreaks?
    \item \textbf{Limitation:} Good calibration doesn't guarantee good discrimination
\end{itemize}

\textbf{ROC \& Thresholds:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Tests discrimination (can model separate positive from negative?)
    \item Identifies optimal decision thresholds for different objectives
    \item Provides sensitivity/specificity trade-off analysis
    \item \textbf{Limitation:} Doesn't assess probability calibration
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Together, these methods provide comprehensive evidence that our models are valid, reliable, and suitable for operational use.}
\end{detail}

