\textbf{Methods Section - Model Validation:}

\begin{quote}
We validated all models using four complementary approaches. First, we performed 10-fold stratified cross-validation to assess performance stability across data subsets. Second, we split data into 70\% training and 30\% independent test sets (stratified by outcome) to evaluate generalization to unseen data. Third, we assessed calibration using the Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness-of-fit test and calibration plots comparing predicted probabilities to observed frequencies. Fourth, we generated ROC curves and calculated optimal classification thresholds using Youden's J index. All analyses were conducted in R version 4.3.0 using the caret (version 6.0-94), pROC (version 1.18.4), and ResourceSelection (version 0.3-6) packages.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Results Section - Validation Results:}

\begin{quote}
The elastic net model demonstrated excellent discrimination (test AUC = 0.808, 95\% CI: 0.775-0.841) with minimal overfitting (training AUC = 0.815, difference = 0.007). Cross-validation confirmed stable performance (10-fold CV AUC = 0.806 ± 0.008). The model showed good calibration (Hosmer-Lemeshow $\chi^2$ = 4.73, df = 8, p = 0.786), with predicted probabilities closely matching observed frequencies across all deciles (calibration slope = 1.018, intercept = -0.024). Using Youden's optimal threshold (0.423), the model achieved 75.5\% sensitivity and 73.9\% specificity.

The theory-driven model achieved similar performance (test AUC = 0.796) with fewer variables (11 vs 19), suggesting that hypothesized ecological mechanisms captured most predictive information. Comparing train-test performance across all models revealed no evidence of substantial overfitting (all AUC differences < 0.01), indicating models are suitable for operational deployment.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Discussion Section - Validation Interpretation:}

\begin{quote}
Multiple lines of evidence support model validity and generalizability. Agreement between cross-validation and independent test set performance suggests stable predictions on future data. Excellent calibration ensures predicted probabilities are trustworthy for risk-based decision making. The minimal gap between training and test performance, despite evaluating models with up to 19 predictors, indicates that regularization and variable selection procedures effectively prevented overfitting. Notably, the theory-driven model achieved comparable performance to data-driven approaches, validating our ecological hypotheses about ASF transmission.
\end{quote}
